  "verbalization": "Osugacestat is implicated in the regulation of VMRC-MELG through a series of molecular interactions involving T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3 and other enzymes. The evidence suggests that Osugacestat acts as a target for multiple compounds, including 2-(1a,4-dimethyl-3,3a,4,5,6,7,7a,7b-octahydro-2H-naphtho[1,2-b]oxiren-7-yl)prop-2-enoic acid and Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, (3beta)-, which both target VMRC-MELG. This indicates that Osugacestat may interact with VMRC-MELG through a pathway involving CD3, which is a key component of T-cell signaling. The presence of multiple targets suggests that Osugacestat may exert its effects through a complex network of molecular interactions, potentially involving enzyme activity or receptor modulation. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of CD3 in mediating the interaction between Osugacestat and VMRC-MELG, underscoring the role of T-cell surface proteins in the overall mechanism of action.",
